-- SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
-- risk: additive
-- (mcp_operation_catalogue restated whole: same signature, same ACL, four
-- operations added; four handlers and three targets are new; no stored state.)
--
-- 0816 (#3937) -- an assistant reads and schedules a member's dated plan
-- changes and pauses, through the commands #3858 (0766) already carries.
--
--   read   list_member_term_changes        a member's scheduled and applied changes
--   write  schedule_member_share_change   a share from a date
--   write  schedule_member_pause          a pause from a date to a date
--   write  cancel_member_term_change      a change not yet applied
--
-- The operations hold none of the commands' rules (the dates, the invoiced
-- periods, the approval): each calls the command under the caller's identity,
-- so the app and the assistant can never disagree about what is allowed. The
-- three writes wait for the person's confirmation in the app; the target asks
-- the command itself first, inside a block that is always rolled back, so a
-- change the command would refuse is never put to the person and nothing is
-- written. A change an approval policy holds answers `pending_validation`: the
-- confirmation authorises the submission, it is not the business's approval.
-- The commands refuse with their own reason (the earliest date allowed, an
-- invoiced period); the answer carries it. Reading is the app's own right:
-- whoever manages members reads any member's changes, a member their own; the
-- table itself refuses a delegated token (mcp_delegated_deny), so the read is
-- this definer function, which asks the right itself.
--
-- A request id answers its own change again (the commands replay it), and a
-- lost answer to a write is returned from the stored outcome by the facade
-- under the same request id and arguments; the same id with other values is
-- refused (request_id_reused). The legacy cancel command is wrapped, not
-- changed: it has no request id of its own.

